      "text": "We start with the given equation:\n\\[\n|n + 6| = 2 - n\n\\]\nThe absolute value function \\( |n + 6| \\) can be split into two cases: \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\) and \\( n + 6 < 0 \\).\n\n### Case 1: \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\)\nWhen \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\), the absolute value expression simplifies directly. We can write:\n\\[\nn + 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\nNow, solve for \\( n \\):\n\\[\nn + 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\nAdd \\( n \\) to both sides:\n\\[\n2n + 6 = 2\n\\]\nSubtract 6 from both sides:\n\\[\n2n = -4\n\\]\nDivide by 2:\n\\[\nn = -2\n\\]\nWe need to check if this solution satisfies the condition \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\):\n\\[\n-2 + 6 = 4 \\geq 0\n\\]\nSince the condition is satisfied, \\( n = -2 \\) is a valid solution for this case.\n\n### Case 2: \\( n + 6 < 0 \\)\nWhen \\( n + 6 < 0 \\), the absolute value expression \\( |n + 6| \\) becomes \\( -(n + 6) \\). We can write:\n\\[\n-(n + 6) = 2 - n\n\\]\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\\[\n-n - 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\nAdd \\( n \\) to both sides:\n\\[\n-6 = 2\n\\]\nThis is a contradiction, so there is no solution in this case.\n\n### Conclusion\nThe only solution that satisfies both conditions is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]",
