      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We are given the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\). To solve this, we need to consider the definition of the absolute value function. The absolute value function \\( |x| \\) is defined as:\n\n\\[\n|x| = \n\\begin{cases} \nx & \\text{if } x \\geq 0 \\\\\n-x & \\text{if } x < 0 \n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nApplying this to our equation, we have two cases to consider: \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\) and \\( n + 6 < 0 \\).\n\n### Case 1: \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\)\nIn this case, \\( |n + 6| = n + 6 \\). Substituting this into the original equation, we get:\n\n\\[\nn + 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( n \\), we first add \\( n \\) to both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\nn + 6 + n = 2 - n + n\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n2n + 6 = 2\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract 6 from both sides:\n\n\\[\n2n + 6 - 6 = 2 - 6\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n2n = -4\n\\]\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by 2:\n\n\\[\nn = \\frac{-4}{2} = -2\n\\]\n\nWe need to verify if \\( n = -2 \\) satisfies the condition \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\):\n\n\\[\n-2 + 6 = 4 \\geq 0\n\\]\n\nThe condition is satisfied, so \\( n = -2 \\) is a valid solution for this case.\n\n### Case 2: \\( n + 6 < 0 \\)\nIn this case, \\( |n + 6| = -(n + 6) \\). Substituting this into the original equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n-(n + 6) = 2 - n\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this, we have:\n\n\\[\n-n - 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\n\nAdding \\( n \\) to both sides:\n\n\\[\n-n - 6 + n = 2 - n + n\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n-6 = 2\n\\]\n\nThis is a contradiction, so there are no solutions in this case.\n\n### Conclusion\nThe only solution that satisfies the original equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\) is \\( n = -2 \\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]",
