      "text": "Starting from the given equation:\n\\[ |n + 6| = 2 - n \\]\n\nWe need to consider the definition of the absolute value function, which tells us that the expression inside the absolute value can be either non-negative or negative. This leads us to two possible cases:\n\n**Case 1: \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\)**\n\nIn this case, \\( |n + 6| = n + 6 \\). The equation becomes:\n\\[ n + 6 = 2 - n \\]\n\nAdding \\( n \\) to both sides:\n\\[ 2n + 6 = 2 \\]\n\nSubtracting 6 from both sides:\n\\[ 2n = -4 \\]\n\nDividing both sides by 2:\n\\[ n = -2 \\]\n\nWe need to check if this solution satisfies the condition \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\):\n\\[ -2 + 6 = 4 \\]\nSince 4 is greater than or equal to 0, \\( n = -2 \\) is a valid solution for this case.\n\n**Case 2: \\( n + 6 < 0 \\)**\n\nIn this case, \\( |n + 6| = -(n + 6) = -n - 6 \\). The equation becomes:\n\\[ -n - 6 = 2 - n \\]\n\nAdding \\( n \\) to both sides:\n\\[ -6 = 2 \\]\n\nAdding 6 to both sides:\n\\[ 0 = 8 \\]\n\nThis is a contradiction, meaning there are no solutions for this case.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is from Case 1, where \\( n = -2 \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{-2} \\]",
