      "text": "We need to solve the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\). To solve this absolute value equation, we need to consider the definition of the absolute value function. The expression \\( |n + 6| \\) represents the distance of \\( n + 6 \\) from 0 on the number line. Thus, we have two cases to consider:\n\n1. \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\)\n2. \\( n + 6 < 0 \\)\n\nLet's solve each case separately:\n\n### Case 1: \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\)\nIn this case, \\( |n + 6| = n + 6 \\). Therefore, the equation becomes:\n\\[\nn + 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( n \\), we will isolate \\( n \\) on one side of the equation:\n\\[\nn + 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\nAdd \\( n \\) to both sides:\n\\[\n2n + 6 = 2\n\\]\nSubtract 6 from both sides:\n\\[\n2n = -4\n\\]\nDivide by 2:\n\\[\nn = -2\n\\]\n\nWe need to check if this solution satisfies the condition \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\):\n\\[\n-2 + 6 = 4 \\geq 0\n\\]\nSince the condition is satisfied, \\( n = -2 \\) is a valid solution for this case.\n\n### Case 2: \\( n + 6 < 0 \\)\nIn this case, \\( |n + 6| = -(n + 6) \\). Therefore, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n-(n + 6) = 2 - n\n\\]\nDistribute the negative sign on the left side:\n\\[\n-n - 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\nAdd \\( n \\) to both sides:\n\\[\n-6 = 2\n\\]\nThis is a contradiction, which means there is no solution for this case.\n\n### Conclusion\nThe only valid solution from the two cases is \\( n = -2 \\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]",
